WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.469
Film Courage: What’s the first step in step
in writing a screenplay?

00:00:03.469 --> 00:00:08.870
Larry Wilson: There’s an idea.

00:00:08.870 --> 00:00:22.279
And usually what I know is…I know…BEETLE
JUICE (for instance) the idea was that I discussed

00:00:22.279 --> 00:00:26.070
with my partners was a psychedelic ghost comedy.

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:32.529
We had no idea what that meant, but it rolled
off the tongue, right?

00:00:32.529 --> 00:00:40.440
And I usually have something of an idea of
that and I kind of know the beginning and

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:42.420
I kind of know the end.

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:46.059
The middle is the great unknown.

00:00:46.059 --> 00:00:50.540
But it’s instinct and an idea and sometimes
it’s a character.

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:52.899
Sometimes it’s a situation.

00:00:52.899 --> 00:00:58.199
But it’s very internal first.

00:00:58.199 --> 00:01:04.790
And yes, there are times where you write on
assignment and you’re given an idea and

00:01:04.790 --> 00:01:07.259
they say translate this into a movie.

00:01:07.259 --> 00:01:18.579
But in terms of my own writing if you will,
my personal writing, it usually comes from

00:01:18.579 --> 00:01:27.179
the most basic kind of idea and a feeling.

00:01:27.179 --> 00:01:31.310
I can’t articulate very well, I’m sorry,
because it’s hard to articulate.

00:01:31.310 --> 00:01:41.429
It’s kind of like a situation or a character
that’s kind of like waiting to come out

00:01:41.429 --> 00:01:48.200
and then whatever your process is…and boy
do you need a process!

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:49.859
And boy do you need to go to work.

00:01:49.859 --> 00:01:57.959
And boy do you need to sit down and do it,
and do the job of writing and you allow that

00:01:57.959 --> 00:02:03.799
character or that situation to just start
growing and just start coming and leaving

00:02:03.799 --> 00:02:10.380
your fingers onto the digital pages, onto
your computer and then it grows and expands

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:12.330
and takes on life.

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:20.180
And of courage, this is every writer’s choice.

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:24.299
There’s a lot of talk about backstory.

00:02:24.299 --> 00:02:29.530
Like…”Tell us, where did your character
go to school?”

00:02:29.530 --> 00:02:32.330
“What was his or her Mommy like?”

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:33.379
And all of that.

00:02:33.379 --> 00:02:38.150
I’ve never written backstory in my life,
never.

00:02:38.150 --> 00:02:44.760
But if you asked me to tell you the backstory
of one of my characters, I could.

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:52.969
I’d kind of be making it up as I went along,
but the feelings would be true.

00:02:52.969 --> 00:02:58.230
Because I feel the characters and I feel like
they need to come out.

00:02:58.230 --> 00:03:05.060
And I feel like they need to be given a voice.

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:14.620
That’s what writing is to me, an idea or
a character or a situation, an emotion or

00:03:14.620 --> 00:03:21.209
a feeling that you some how have to put into
the world.

00:03:21.209 --> 00:03:24.930
And it’s hard to articulate and I feel like
I am talking in circles.

00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:35.040
But it’s almost hard not to because it’s
so ephemeral in the beginning.

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:37.629
I hope that makes some version of sense?

00:03:37.629 --> 00:03:42.959
Film Courage: It does…so the middle it sounds
like is undetermined, but you know an ending

00:03:42.959 --> 00:03:43.959
when you start something?

00:03:43.959 --> 00:03:48.379
Larry Wilson: I kind of know an ending, I
kind of know an ending, but that can all change.

00:03:48.379 --> 00:03:55.440
But people get very scared of the middle but
that’s when it gets fun because it’s so

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:57.359
challenging.

00:03:57.359 --> 00:04:06.129
And you so get to …if you think of a screenplay
as 110, 112 pages, you get to page, you get

00:04:06.129 --> 00:04:09.620
to page 60 and you go “Oh my Gosh!

00:04:09.620 --> 00:04:12.569
I’m only half way there.

00:04:12.569 --> 00:04:14.590
What happens next?”

00:04:14.590 --> 00:04:25.330
But something will happen and that’s exhilarating
to go to bed completely sure that you will

00:04:25.330 --> 00:04:32.150
never figure out what’s going to happen
next and wake up with the answer.

00:04:32.150 --> 00:04:38.440
And it’s a daily challenge.

00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:50.460
But if feels so good to realize that next
scene will be there if you keep working at

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:51.460
it.

00:04:51.460 --> 00:04:53.800
It’s the best feeling in the world.

00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:55.009
Other than finishing it.

00:04:55.009 --> 00:05:01.090
Finishing the first draft no matter what shape
it’s in is like brilliant.

00:05:01.090 --> 00:05:08.449
I always get tears in my eyes when I finish
a first draft because I feel like I am saying

00:05:08.449 --> 00:05:15.590
goodbye to these people who I met on this
journey.

00:05:15.590 --> 00:05:19.240
And there’s a very emotional side to it.

00:05:19.240 --> 00:05:24.310
And then you become the craftsman and time
for a second draft.

00:05:24.310 --> 00:05:31.610
But it’s an emotional journey and to be
able to do this day-after-day, it’s rewarding.

00:05:31.610 --> 00:05:34.750
I have the best job in the world.

00:05:34.750 --> 00:05:41.410
Film Courage: So let’s say you are 60 pages
in and you’re like “What do I do next?”

00:05:41.410 --> 00:05:46.730
So keeping in the vein of not waiting for
inspiration and this is a job.

00:05:46.730 --> 00:05:48.460
It’s like working at a bank.

00:05:48.460 --> 00:05:49.530
So what’s happening?

00:05:49.530 --> 00:05:54.040
Let’s suppose it’s 9 a.m. on Monday morning,
you’re 60 pages in, you’ve got the cup

00:05:54.040 --> 00:05:56.800
of coffee and you are there and …?

00:05:56.800 --> 00:06:03.360
Larry Wilson: I will go back.

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:07.979
I will never go back to the beginning because
that is a death trap.

00:06:07.979 --> 00:06:09.310
“You know what I’ll do?

00:06:09.310 --> 00:06:11.830
I’ll go back and rewrite the first 60 pages!

00:06:11.830 --> 00:06:15.150
And then I’ll go back and rewrite the first
60 pages again and again.”

00:06:15.150 --> 00:06:20.530
I’ll go back and read a couple of pages
and then go in and do some cosmetic stuff

00:06:20.530 --> 00:06:28.710
just to put myself back into the zone of the
story.

00:06:28.710 --> 00:06:33.940
And then I just start writing and they may
not be good ideas.

00:06:33.940 --> 00:06:40.220
My drafts are always 20 to 30 pages too long,
when I finish the first draft.

00:06:40.220 --> 00:06:46.140
Because I am telling the story to myself.

00:06:46.140 --> 00:06:47.140
I forget who it was?

00:06:47.140 --> 00:06:48.550
I’m going to say Oscar Wilde.

00:06:48.550 --> 00:06:54.210
I’m going to say some famous writer said
“Just sit down and start typing your name

00:06:54.210 --> 00:06:55.210
over and over again.”

00:06:55.210 --> 00:06:59.590
Oscar Wilde may be pre-typwriter but you understand
the point.

00:06:59.590 --> 00:07:01.199
Start typing your name.

00:07:01.199 --> 00:07:04.159
Start doing anything with your fingers.

00:07:04.159 --> 00:07:16.370
And it’s like where that comes from and
where that next scene come from, it’s there.

00:07:16.370 --> 00:07:26.620
It is there and if you put yourself in the
position to find it, you will find it.

00:07:26.620 --> 00:07:34.729
And I might spend a day writing 10 basically
bad pages but there will be one page of truth

00:07:34.729 --> 00:07:42.210
in it, one moment that will move the story
forward.

00:07:42.210 --> 00:07:51.240
And Stephen King talks about this really brilliantly
and I have questions about outlining and I’m

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:54.020
not here to have an outline debate.

00:07:54.020 --> 00:08:01.060
He’s scathing about outlining if you’ve
ever read his book On Writing, it’s probably

00:08:01.060 --> 00:08:07.539
the best book on writing in my mind, but he
always says “It’s what happens next.

00:08:07.539 --> 00:08:11.789
It’s what happens next, what happens next,
what happens next.”

00:08:11.789 --> 00:08:21.009
And if I were to reach across the room here
and steal your notes from you, there would

00:08:21.009 --> 00:08:22.009
be a response!

00:08:22.009 --> 00:08:23.110
Film Courage: [Laughs] Yes…

00:08:23.110 --> 00:08:27.979
Larry Wilson: Or if I were to kick the camera
over and say “I’m done with this interview!”

00:08:27.979 --> 00:08:29.919
There would be a response, right?

00:08:29.919 --> 00:08:33.220
And then it’s what happens next?

00:08:33.220 --> 00:08:38.130
Do you jump on me and say “Give me my notes
back you jerk!”

00:08:38.130 --> 00:08:49.790
I mean and if you just look at it like that…and
you look at it like whatever you make happen,

00:08:49.790 --> 00:08:52.010
something has to happen next and “It’s
what happens next.

00:08:52.010 --> 00:08:55.520
It’s what happens next, what happens next,
what happens next.”

00:08:55.520 --> 00:08:58.940
That what happens next will come and it may
not happen easily.

00:08:58.940 --> 00:09:05.770
It may be a lot of bad what happens next before
you find it.

00:09:05.770 --> 00:09:14.000
But it will come and it’s a magical process,
and magical in the sense that it’s almost

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:16.400
unexplainable.

00:09:16.400 --> 00:09:24.000
And it’s why there are so many screenwriting
programs and apps and all these things out

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:28.650
there to kind of tell you that they can tell
you what happens next.

00:09:28.650 --> 00:09:33.220
But they can’t tell you what happens next,
only you can tell what happens next.

00:09:33.220 --> 00:09:39.480
And I look at it as a moment-by-moment process
and I put these characters in a situation

00:09:39.480 --> 00:09:47.960
and it’s often diabolical and it’s often
otherworldly and they get themselves deeper

00:09:47.960 --> 00:09:50.940
into it or get the hell out.

00:09:50.940 --> 00:09:57.460
And that’s the journey and it’s moment-by-moment
and it’s sort of literally second-by-second,

00:09:57.460 --> 00:09:59.100
if you think about.

00:09:59.100 --> 00:10:10.390
You know, movies move forward in a linear
fashion and it’s just tracking that and

00:10:10.390 --> 00:10:15.080
when it really gets fun is when you put your
character in the most…here can I tell you

00:10:15.080 --> 00:10:22.100
a quick anecdote that is probably an illustration
that I use all the time…

